articles.description:
In-browser code editors show up in a lot of products: a SQL workbench, a policy playground, a "try our language" widget, a low-code formula bar. Monaco and CodeMirror make that feel like VS Code in a tab.
The catch is that any grammar, completion, or lint rule the editor can apply without a server has to be in the page. This article is about what a client-side language package actually exposes, which checks should stay on a backend, and how to protect a proprietary analyzer without wrapping the editor shell.
